  "query_preview": "/MONDAY THROUGH FRIDAY/...\nIssued at 120 PM MDT Sat Sep 26 2026\n\nMonday...Model solutions are staying somewhat consistent and still \non track. The upper ridge over the central US weakens even more and \ncontinues to push to the east. The incoming Pacific closed low \nsystem strengthens as it drops south across CA, while at the same \ntime Hurricane Polo moves onshore across the CA Baja and into \nMexico, feeding abundant moisture up into the Desert SW. Model \ntrends indicate that by Monday morning precip chances jump up \nconsiderably along the Continental Divide, then these high chances \nthen spread from west to east through the day, increasing to \nscattered pops for the eastern plains by the night. This trend is \nthe same as what was offered yesterday, though QPF amounts are \nstarting to be more focused, with around an inch of rain for he SW \nmts through Monday, .3 to .5 of an inch for the rest of the \nContinental Divide and Sangres, and then less than a tenth for the \nrest of the area. WPC has a Slight area for excessive rainfall over \nthe Four Corners nosing into the SW mts, but has indicated that they \nwill increase this area to a Moderate. Therefore, a Flash Flood \nWatch for the SW mts will be likely in the near future, though \nwanted to hold off at this time to allow following shifts another \nlook. As for temps, increased cloud cover and incoming precip will \ncool the higher elevations and valleys, but it should be one more \nwarm day across the eastern plains. Therefore, look for highs in the \n60s for the high valleys, and mid 70s to lower 80s for the plains.\n\nTuesday through Thursday...Better agreement between the long range \nmodels for mid-week now, as the closed upper low absorbs the \nremnants of Hurricane Polo and track s east across AZ on Tue, NM on \nWed, then northeast over the OK and TX panhandles on Thu. Latest QPF \namounts over this 3 day period are showing a range of 1.5 to nearly \n4 inches of rain, focusing on the eastern mts and plains, roughly \nfrom Pueblo and Crowley counties southward. Additional Flash Flood \nWatches may be necessary, especially for those areas already \nexperiencing saturated grounds. This will be a cooler period through \nthe extended due to extensive cloud cover and ongoing precip, with \nhighs each day in the 60s for the high valleys, and 60s to lower 70s \nfor the plains.\n\nFriday...The NBM warms Friday a few degrees as the system exits the \narea and precip chances dwindle. This will be highly dependent on \ntrack of the upper low, with some models hinting at the low \nlingering in the area for another 24 hours. Since this is identical \nto what models indicated for Thu, just yesterday, this will likely \ngo through additional changes. Moore",
